t**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) occur, and the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) is a right‑isosceles lattice triangle with vertices  \n   \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \n   Its three sides contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   V_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   H_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   D_n=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (vertical, horizontal and diagonal sides).  \n   The three sides together contain \\(3n-3\\) distinct points.\n\n2. **Upper bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any admissible configuration at least two of the lines must be **non‑sunny** (i.e. parallel to one of the three sides).  \n   *Proof idea.*  \n   A sunny line can meet each side of the triangle in **at most one** point, because a line parallel to a side would be non‑sunny.  \n   Suppose only one non‑sunny line were present. That line can be parallel to at most one side, covering all \\(n\\) points of that side. On each of the other two sides we would have at most \\(k+1\\) covered points (the \\(k\\) sunny lines each contribute at most one, plus the possible intersection of the single non‑sunny line). Since each of those sides contains \\(n\\) points, we would need  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   But then the total number of lines would be  \n\n   \\[\n   n=k+1\\ge (n-1)+1=n,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all lines would be sunny except the one non‑sunny line, giving **exactly** \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   In that situation the two sides that are not parallel to the non‑sunny line would each have **exactly** \\(n\\) points, of which \\(n-