 equivalently a sunny line is not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nA sunny line can intersect each side of the triangle in **at most one** point, because if it met a side in two distinct points it would be parallel to that side, contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nConsequently, on each side the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can be parallel to **exactly one** side; it then contains all \\(n\\) points of that side, and can intersect each of the other two sides in at most one point.\n\nHence, on any given side the number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}+\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{if a non‑sunny line is parallel to this side}}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nk+1\\quad\\text{(if no non‑sunny line is parallel to this side)} .\n\\]\n\nSince each side contains exactly \\(n\\) points, at least one non‑sunny line must be parallel to each side **unless** the sunny lines already supply the missing points.  \nIf a side were covered only by sunny lines, we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines for that side, which would already give \\(k\\ge n\\), impossible because the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore **at least two** of the lines must be non‑sunny; otherwise we would need \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines to cover the two sides that are not parallel to the unique non‑sunny line, and the two uncovered points on those sides would have to coincide, which cannot happen.  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;x=1\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n\\qquad(\\text{diagonal}).\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the whole side \\(V_n\\); \\(\\ell_2\\) contains the whole side \\(D_n\\).\n\n2. **Lines through the horizontal side**  \n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(s_i\\) equals \\(-1\\); therefore \\(s_i\\) is **non‑sunny**.  \n   The point \\((i,1)\\) lies on the horizontal side \\(H_n\\).\n\n   Replace a chosen subset of these lines by sunny ones as follows.  \n   For any \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny and still passes through the horizontal point \\((i,1)\\).\n\n3. **Choosing the sunny lines**  \n\n   Select a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in S\\,\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\notin S\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n   * the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (one for each \\(i\\in S\\));  \n   * the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(s_i\\) (for \\(i\\notin S\\)).  \n\n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=2+k+(n-2-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering property**  \n\n   *Vertical side* \\(V_n\\) – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n\n   *Diagonal side* \\(D_n\\) – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_2\\).  \n\n   *Horizontal side* \\(H_n\\) – for each \\(i\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered either by \\(\\sigma_i\\) (if \\(i\\in S\\)) or by \\(s_i\\) (if \\(i\\notin S\\)).  \n\n   *Interior points.*  \n   Any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) belongs to the line \\(s_a\\) (or to \\(\\sigma_a\\) when \\(a\\in S\\)), because \\(s_a\\) is precisely the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,a)\\); the segment joining these two vertices contains all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le a\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nInequality (1) proved in Section 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
