## Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) occur, and the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) is a right‑isosceles lattice triangle with vertices  \n   \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \n   Its three sides contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   V_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   H_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   D_n=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (vertical, horizontal and diagonal sides).  \n   The three sides together contain \\(3n-3\\) distinct points.\n\n2. **Upper bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any admissible configuration at least two of the lines must be **non‑sunny** (i.e. parallel to one of the three sides).  \n   *Proof idea.*  \n   A sunny line can meet each side of the triangle in **at most one** point, because a line parallel to a side would be non‑sunny.  \n   Suppose only one non‑sunny line were present. That line can be parallel to at most one side, covering all \\(n\\) points of that side. On each of the other two sides we would have at most \\(k+1\\) covered points (the \\(k\\) sunny lines each contribute at most one, plus the possible intersection of the single non‑sunny line). Since each of those sides contains \\(n\\) points, we would need  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   But then the total number of lines would be  \n\n   \\[\n   n=k+1\\ge (n-1)+1=n,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all lines would be sunny except the one non‑sunny line, giving **exactly** \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   In that situation the two sides that are not parallel to the non‑sunny line would each have **exactly** \\(n\\) points, of which \\(n-1\\) are supplied by the sunny lines and the remaining one by the intersection of the non‑sunny line. Consequently the two sides would share the *same* intersection point, which is impossible because the three sides meet pairwise only at the three vertices. Hence a configuration with only one non‑sunny line cannot exist.  \n   Therefore at least **two** non‑sunny lines are necessary, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 2.* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Start with the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical)} ,\\qquad\n     \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n\\qquad\\text{(diagonal)} .\n     \\]\n\n     These two lines already contain all points of the vertical side \\(V_n\\) and of the diagonal side \\(D_n\\).  \n   - The horizontal side \\(H_n\\) still has the \\(n-2\\) interior points  \n\n     \\[\n     (2,1),(3,1),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\n     \\]\n\n     uncovered.  \n   - For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n     \\[\n     s_i:\\;\\text{the unique line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i).\n     \\]\n\n     Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i-1}{1-i}= -1\\), **so it is non‑sunny**.  \n     Instead we replace it by the **sunny** line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i+1).\n     \\]\n\n     The two points are distinct, the slope equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i+1-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{i}{i-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \n     Moreover \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\) of the horizontal side, so it covers that point.  \n   - Choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \n     Replace the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) (which would be \\(s_i\\) if we had kept the whole family) by the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) for each \\(i\\in S\\).  \n     The resulting family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_k=\n     \\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\bigr\\}\n     \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in S\\,\\}\n     \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\notin S\\,\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: the two original non‑sunny lines, the \\(k\\) sunny replacements, and the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(s_i\\).  \n\n   - **Coverage.**  \n     *Vertical side* – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n     *Diagonal side* – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_2\\).  \n     *Horizontal side* – the points \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\in S\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\); the points with \\(i\\notin S\\) are covered by the non‑sunny lines \\(s_i\\).  \n     *Interior points* – every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(s_i\\) (or \\(\\sigma_i\\)) because each such line is the line through the two vertices of the small right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(i,1),(1,i)\\). Hence all interior points are covered as well.  \n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the two required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   From Lemma 1 we already have the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe three sides of the triangle \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_n&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nH_n&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nD_n&=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three sides; equivalently a sunny line is not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nA sunny line can intersect each side of the triangle in **at most one** point, because if it met a side in two distinct points it would be parallel to that side, contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nConsequently, on each side the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can be parallel to **exactly one** side; it then contains all \\(n\\) points of that side, and can intersect each of the other two sides in at most one point.\n\nHence, on any given side the number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}+\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{if a non‑sunny line is parallel to this side}}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nk+1\\quad\\text{(if no non‑sunny line is parallel to this side)} .\n\\]\n\nSince each side contains exactly \\(n\\) points, at least one non‑sunny line must be parallel to each side **unless** the sunny lines already supply the missing points.  \nIf a side were covered only by sunny lines, we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines for that side, which would already give \\(k\\ge n\\), impossible because the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore **at least two** of the lines must be non‑sunny; otherwise we would need \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines to cover the two sides that are not parallel to the unique non‑sunny line, and the two uncovered points on those sides would have to coincide, which cannot happen.  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;x=1\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n\\qquad(\\text{diagonal}).\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the whole side \\(V_n\\); \\(\\ell_2\\) contains the whole side \\(D_n\\).\n\n2. **Lines through the horizontal side**  \n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(s_i\\) equals \\(-1\\); therefore \\(s_i\\) is **non‑sunny**.  \n   The point \\((i,1)\\) lies on the horizontal side \\(H_n\\).\n\n   Replace a chosen subset of these lines by sunny ones as follows.  \n   For any \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny and still passes through the horizontal point \\((i,1)\\).\n\n3. **Choosing the sunny lines**  \n\n   Select a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in S\\,\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\notin S\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n   * the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (one for each \\(i\\in S\\));  \n   * the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(s_i\\) (for \\(i\\notin S\\)).  \n\n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=2+k+(n-2-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering property**  \n\n   *Vertical side* \\(V_n\\) – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n\n   *Diagonal side* \\(D_n\\) – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_2\\).  \n\n   *Horizontal side* \\(H_n\\) – for each \\(i\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered either by \\(\\sigma_i\\) (if \\(i\\in S\\)) or by \\(s_i\\) (if \\(i\\notin S\\)).  \n\n   *Interior points.*  \n   Any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) belongs to the line \\(s_a\\) (or to \\(\\sigma_a\\) when \\(a\\in S\\)), because \\(s_a\\) is precisely the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,a)\\); the segment joining these two vertices contains all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le a\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nInequality (1) proved in Section 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
