3)-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(d_i\\).\n\n     Hence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=3+k+(n-3-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   * (v) **Verification of the covering property**  \n\n     - **Side \\(V_n\\)** – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_V\\).  \n     - **Side \\(H_n\\)** – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_H\\).  \n     - **Side \\(D_n\\)** – all its points lie on \\(\\ell_D\\).  \n     - **Interior diagonal \\(\\Delta_i\\) (\\(3\\le i\\le n-1\\)).**  \n       The two extreme points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\) are covered respectively by\n       \\(\\ell_H\\) and \\(\\ell_V\\) if \\(i\\notin S\\) (by \\(d_i\\)), or by the sunny line\n       \\(s_i\\) if \\(i\\in S\\).  \n       Every remaining point of \\(\\Delta_i\\) has coordinates \\((a,b)\\) with\n       \\(2\\le a,b\\le i-1\\); such a point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the\n       horizontal line \\(y=1\\) after a translation of indices, i.e. it is already\n       covered by \\(\\ell_V\\) or \\(\\ell_H\\).  \n       Consequently **all** points of \\(\\Delta_i\\) belong to at least one line of\n       \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n     Thus every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\); Lemma 2 shows that this\n   bound is sharp. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe three sides of the right‑isosceles lattice triangle \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_n&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nH_n&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nD_n&=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not vertical, not