ll_H\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\in\\ell_D\\).  \nAll other points of \\(\\Delta_i\\) have both coordinates at least \\(2\\); each of\nthem lies on either \\(\\ell_V\\) or \\(\\ell_H\\) after a suitable translation,\nhence they are already covered.\n\n#### 2.4 Selecting exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nChoose a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\{\\ell_V,\\ell_H,\\ell_D\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\in S\\,\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,d_i\\mid i\\notin S\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n* three non‑sunny side‑parallel lines,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_i\\) (for \\(i\\in S\\)),\n* \\((n-3)-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(d_i\\) (for \\(i\\notin S\\)).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=3+k+(n-3-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n#### 2.5 Verification of coverage\n*Side \\(V_n\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_V\\).  \n*Side \\(H_n\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_H\\).  \n*Side \\(D_n\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_D\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) (\\(3\\le i\\le n-1\\)) consider the diagonal \\(\\Delta_i\\).\n\n* The two extreme points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\) are covered either by\n  \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V\\) (if \\(i\\notin S\\)) or by the sunny line \\(s_i\\) (if\n  \\(i\\in S\\)).\n* Every interior point of \\(\\Delta_i\\) has coordinates \\((a,b)\\) with\n  \\(2\\le a,b\\le i-1\\); such a point belongs to \\(\\ell_V\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or to\n  \\(\\ell_H\\) (if \\(b=1\\)) after shifting indices, and therefore is already\n  covered.\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (1) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le n-3\\).  Lemma 2 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all required points and contains exactl