\ell_{D2}: x+y=n   \\;(\\text{also slope }-1).\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_{V},\\ell_{H},\\ell_{D1},\\ell_{D2}\\) respectively contain the whole sets\n\\(V_{n},H_{n},D_{n}\\) and the diagonal \\(\\{(a,n-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}\\).\nAll four are non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  Diagonal lines for the remaining interior sums  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_{i}:\\text{ line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i)\n\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d_{i}\\) contains the whole lattice diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{i}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=i+1\\}\n          =\\{(i,1),(i-1,2),\\dots ,(1,i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(d_{i}\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny replacements  \n\nChoose an arbitrary subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{4,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).\nFor each \\(i\\in S\\) replace the line \\(d_{i}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns_{i}:\\text{ line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(2,2).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(s_{i}\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{2-i}= \\frac{1}{2-i}\\),\nwhich is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(i\\ge 4\\).  Hence each\n\\(s_{i}\\) is sunny and distinct (different \\(i\\) give different slopes).\n\n#### 3.4  The final family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{\\ell_{V},\\ell_{H},\\ell_{D1},\\ell_{D2}\\}\n                \\cup\\{\\,s_{i}\\mid i\\in S\\,\\}\n                \\cup\\{\\,d_{i}\\mid i\\notin S,\\;3\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nCounting the lines:\n\n* 4 fixed non‑sunny lines,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_{i}\\),\n* \\((n-4)-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals \\(d_{i}\\) (the indices \\(i=3\\) and the\n  \\(n-4\\) values \\(4,\\dots ,n-1\\) give a total of \\(n-4\\) diagonals).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=4+k+(n-4-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n#### 3.5  Verification of the covering property  \n\n*Side \\(V_{n}\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_{V}\\).  \n*Side \\(H_{n}\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_{H}\\).  \n*Side \\(D_{n}\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_{D1}\\).  \n*Diagonal \\(x+y=n\\):* all its points lie on \\(\\ell_{D2}\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le n-1\\) consider the diagonal \\(\\Delta_{i}\\).\n\n* If \\(i\\notin S\\) we keep \\(d_{i}\\); then \\(\\Delta_{i}\\subseteq d_{i}\\).\n* If \\(i\\in S\\) we have removed \\(d_{i}\\) and inserted \\(s_{i}\\).  \n  The two extreme points of \\(\\Delta_{i}\\) are \\((i,1)\\) (on \\(\\ell_{H}\\)) and\n  \\((1,i)\\) (on \\(\\ell_{V}\\)).  \n  The interior points of \\(\\Delta_{i}\\) all have coordinates \\(\\ge 2\\); each of\n  them lies on the sunny line \\(s_{i}\\) because \\(s_{i}\\) passes through\n  \\((2,2)\\) and \\((i,1)\\) and therefore contains every lattice point of the\n  form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  (Indeed, the equation of\n  \\(s_{i}\\) is \\( (b-2) = \\frac{1}{2-i}(a-2)\\), which is satisfied exactly by\n  those points.)\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe inequality \\(k\\le n-4\\) (and \\(k=0\\) for \\(n=3\\)) is forced by the\nnecessity of four non‑sunny lines.  The construction above shows that every\ninteger \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-4\\) can indeed be realized.  Therefore the\ncomplete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-4\\}}\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 4,\n\\qquad\\boxed{\\{0\\}}\\quad\\text{for }n=3 .\n\\]"
