 the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2.1) the two mandatory lines already cover \\((1,1)\\) and the two points\n\\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  Thus at most one further line is needed, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\nFor \\(n=4\\) the points that are **not** on \\(\\ell_{v}\\cup\\ell_{h}\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(2,2),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,2),\\;(3,3),\\;(4,1),\\;(1,4).\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can meet each of the three sides \\(V_{4},H_{4},D_{4}\\) in at most one lattice\npoint, so with only two sunny lines we could cover at most four of the six points above.\nHence at least three sunny lines are necessary and, together with the two mandatory\nnon‑sunny lines, we would have more than four lines.  Consequently for \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus (2.2) is sharp for \\(n\\ge5\\) but not for \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  A family of sunny lines that works for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nFor every integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; \\text{the line through }(i,n+1-i)\\in D_{n}\n\\text{ and }(1,i-1)\\in H_{n}\\;}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)-(i-1)}{i-1}= \\frac{\\,n+2-2i\\,}{\\,i-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have  \n\n* \\(i-1\\neq0\\)  → \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\);\n* \\(n+2-2i\\neq0\\)  → \\(m_i\\neq0\\);\n* \\(m_i=-1\\) would give \\(n+2-2i=-(i-1)\\), i.e. \\(i=n+1\\), impossible.\n\nHence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is **sunny**.  \nMoreover \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the diagonal point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and also the horizontal\npoint \\((1,i-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)  \n\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}}_{\\text{three non‑sunny lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_i\\mid i\\in